<section id="the-constitution-and-separation-of-powers" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="the-constitution-and-separation-of-powers">The Constitution and separation of powers</h2>
<p>The US Constitution (1787) splits power between three branches that check each other:</p>
<table class="caption-top table">
<colgroup>
<col style="width: 33%">
<col style="width: 33%">
<col style="width: 33%">
</colgroup>
<thead>
<tr class="header">
<th>Branch</th>
<th>Who</th>
<th>How they get there</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Legislative</td>
<td>Congress: Senate (100) + House of Representatives (435)</td>
<td>Elected: senators for 6 years, representatives for 2</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>Executive</td>
<td>President</td>
<td>Elected every 4 years, at most two terms</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td>Judicial</td>
<td>Supreme Court (9 justices)</td>
<td>Nominated by the president, confirmed by the Senate, serve for life</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
